# vip9000.note — NBG conversion takeaways from on-board debugging (A733 / Cubie A7A)

Audience: the person regenerating `kata1-b10c128.a733.bs{1,4}.fp16/network_binary.nb`.

This is what the on-board VIPLite runtime sees and what it actually needs.
The C++ backend that consumes the NBG (`src/vip9000_compute.cpp` on
`multi-gpu`) is in place and verified up to (but not including) the call
into `vip_create_network`; the only blocker is that the currently-checked-in
NBGs reject at load time. This note is the data the converter side needs.

> **2026-04-29 amendment.** §4.2 below claims the on-board runtime
> requires NBG format version `0x0001001E`. That turned out to be
> wrong: Allwinner's own v3 sample at
> `ai-sdk/examples/yolact/model/v3/yolact.nb` (target = `0x1000003B`,
> the same A733) carries NBG format `0x00020000`, identical to what
> Acuity v6.30.22 in the documented `ubuntu-npu:v2.0.10.1` Docker
> image produces. Other v3 samples (vpm_run, resnet50, yolov5) use
> `0x1001E` or `0x10020`. Both v1.x and v2.x NBGs load on viplite
> v2.0.3+ as long as the target byte is right. The
> `nbglk_valid_nbg_check` rejection in §3a was driven solely by
> `target=0x15`. Treat §4.2 below as historical.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 1. Hardware ground-truth on the Cubie A7A

The Allwinner A733's NPU reports:

```
$ cat /sys/kernel/debug/viplite/vip_info
dev0 hw0 info: pid=0x1000003b, date=0x20230518, ver1=0x9000, ver2=0x9202.
```

VIPLite confirms the same on every `vip_init`:

```
VIPLite driver software version 2.0.3.2-AW-2024-08-30
VIPLite driver version=0x00020003
cid=0x1000003b, device_count=1
  device[0] core_count=1
```

So the **target chip ID the runtime expects in any loaded NBG is
`0x1000003B`** (VIP9000 NanoDI+, with the `_PLUS` revision encoded in the
top nibble `0x10000000`). One NPU device, one core. The on-board
viplite v2.0.3.2-AW-2024-08-30 is the `viplite-tina` cut from
`github.com/ZIFENG278/ai-sdk` at
`viplite-tina/lib/aarch64-none-linux-gnu/v2.0/`.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 2. What the current bs=1 / bs=4 NBGs actually contain

```
$ xxd models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb | head -1
00000000: 5650 4d4e 0500 0200 1500 0000 4549 23a8  VPMN........EI#.
$ xxd models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs4.fp16/network_binary.nb | head -1
00000000: 5650 4d4e 0500 0200 1500 0000 014c 2cf6  VPMN.........L,.
```

NBG header layout (12-byte preamble, little-endian):

| offset | bytes              | value         | meaning                          |
|--------|--------------------|---------------|----------------------------------|
| 0..3   | `56 50 4d 4e`      | `VPMN`        | magic — OK                       |
| 4..7   | `05 00 02 00`      | `0x00020005`  | NBG **format version**           |
| 8..11  | `15 00 00 00`      | `0x00000015`  | **target chip ID** — ❌ wrong    |

Compare to the SDK's known-good v3 sample `ai-sdk/examples/vpm_run/operator/v3/network_binary.nb`:

```
00000000: 5650 4d4e 1e00 0100 3b00 0010 5368 7566  VPMN....;...Shuf
                ^^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^
                version     target_id
                0x0001001E  0x1000003B   ← what the runtime wants
```

Both numbers differ:

* **Format version** `0x00020005` vs `0x0001001E` — older NBG format from
  an older (or just *different*) Acuity / acuitylite version.
* **Target chip ID** `0x00000015` vs `0x1000003B` — the converter wrote a
  short low-byte-only family ID (looks like a generic VIP9000 Nano default),
  not the full PID for the NanoDI+ revision the A733 carries.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 3. How the runtime fails on these NBGs (so you can spot regressions fast)

### 3a. Allwinner's own `vpm_run` against `kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb`

```
$ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/root/proj/ai-sdk/viplite-tina/lib/aarch64-none-linux-gnu/v2.0
$ /root/proj/ai-sdk/examples/vpm_run/install/etc/npu/vpm_run/vpm_run -s sample.txt -l 1
init vip lite, driver version=0x00020003...
VIPLite driver software version 2.0.3.2-AW-2024-08-30
vip lite init OK.
cid=0x1000003b, device_count=1
  device[0] core_count=1
config file read network count=1
init test resources, task_count: 1 ...
create/prepare networks ...
task i=0, binary name: ./kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb
[0xaab16750]nbglk_valid_nbg_check[920], binary target=0x15, actually target=0x1000003B
[0xaab16750]vip_create_network[193], fail to create network status=-4
[0xaab16750]viphal_os_call_drv[294], fail to ioctl vipcore, command[28]:VIPDRV_SET_TASK_PROPERTY, status=-3
[0xaab16750]viphal_task_set_property[1492], fail to check task set property status=-3
Network creating failed. Please validate the content of ./kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb.
create network 0 failed.
vpm run ret=-1
```

The line that matters:

```
nbglk_valid_nbg_check[920], binary target=0x15, actually target=0x1000003B
```

This is the validator inside `libNBGlinker.so` rejecting the NBG **before**
any compute happens. status=-4 is `VIP_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENTS`.

### 3b. The C++ backend gives the same diagnosis up-front

`src/vip9000_compute.cpp` parses the 12-byte NBG header itself and refuses
to call `vip_create_network` if the chip ID doesn't match what
`vip_query_hardware(VIP_QUERY_HW_PROP_CID)` reports:

```
VIP9000 NBG header: target=0x15 version=0x20005 bytes=6493872
NNEvaluator thread 0 (gpu 0): VIP9000: NBG target chip 0x15 does not match
this hardware's CID 0x1000003b.
  NBG path: models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb
```

This is a soft early-warning; even if you bypassed it, `vip_create_network`
would fail anyway (we tried — see §6 "Things that don't work").

### 3c. What success looks like

The SDK's `ai-sdk/examples/vpm_run/operator/v3/network_binary.nb` (a
properly-targeted ResNet-style classifier) loads and runs cleanly on this
hardware:

```
cid=0x1000003b, device_count=1
create network 0: 1944 us.
input 0 dim 224 224 3 1, data_format=2, ...
prepare network 0: 1064 us.
profile inference time=2855us, cycle=2853769
vpm run ret=0
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 4. What a correctly-targeted kata1 NBG must satisfy

Hard requirements (validated against working v3 NBGs in `ai-sdk/examples/`):

1. **NBG header bytes 8..11 = `3B 00 00 10`** (i.e. target_id = `0x1000003B`).
2. **NBG header bytes 4..7 ≈ `1E 00 01 00`** (format version `0x0001001E`)
   — match the version the on-board v2.0.3.2 viplite expects.  Mismatching
   format versions trip the same `nbglk_valid_nbg_check` path even when
   chip IDs match.
   ⚠ **Superseded — see the 2026-04-29 amendment at the top of this file.
   `ai-sdk/examples/yolact/model/v3/yolact.nb` ships with `0x00020000` and
   loads fine; v6.30.22 in the documented Docker image emits the same
   format. Treat 0x1001E vs 0x20000 as toolkit-build-dependent, not as a
   hard runtime requirement.**
3. The NBG must come out of an Acuity build that targets
   **`VIP9000NANODI_PLUS_PID0X1000003B`** (the Allwinner-published
   simulator config for "v3" — A733/A737 variant).  Use Allwinner's
   `pegasus_setup.sh v3` env or feed the env vars directly:
   ```
   export VSIMULATOR_SHADER_CORE_COUNT=1
   export VSIMULATOR_CONFIG=VIP9000NANODI_PLUS_PID0X1000003B
   ```
   ⚠ **In Acuity v6.30.22 / `ubuntu-npu:v2.0.10.1` the actual `.config`
   file shipped under
   `Vivante_IDE/VivanteIDE5.11.0/cmdtools/common/cfg/` is named
   `VIP9000NANODI_PID0X1000003B.config` (no `_PLUS_`). Setting
   `VSIMULATOR_CONFIG=VIP9000NANODI_PLUS_PID0X1000003B` makes the
   toolkit fail to find the chip table. Use the no-`_PLUS_` name; the
   `customerID = 0x1000003b` baked into the resulting NBG is identical
   either way.**

Soft requirements (existing converter is correct on these — keep them):

* Static batch shape (NBG codegen needs it). bs=1 for live play, bs=4 for
  the self-play queue. The C++ backend pads slots `[N..K)` with zeros and
  drops their outputs, so any incoming batch ≤ K is fine.
* `dtype="float"` / no quantize step. fp16 is the VIP9000 native MAC path
  — the existing `pack_nbg_only=True` path is right.
* Inputs: `state_spatial[K,22,9,9]` (NCHW) and `state_global[K,19]`,
  both fp16. `unshare-initializers` mitigation must still be applied to
  the source ONNX (see `A733_CONVERSION.md §3.2`).
* Outputs (in this order, matching `kata_export_for_rknn.py`):
  `policy_logits[K,82]`, `value[K,1]`, `score_mean[K,1]`,
  `score_stdev[K,1]`, `ownership[K,81]`, all fp16.

`models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/nbg_meta.json` from the existing
build is structurally correct (shapes, dtypes, names) — only the
underlying `network_binary.nb` is mis-targeted. You can keep the meta.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 5. Concrete things to try on the conversion host (in priority order)

The host machine **must be x86_64 Linux** for any of these — VeriSilicon
ships Acuity / acuitylite as x86_64-only binaries.

### Path A — switch off pip `acuitylite` to Allwinner's official Acuity Toolkit

Radxa documents this as the canonical path. Pull their Docker image:

```bash
docker pull radxa/ubuntu-npu:v2.0.10.1   # name from Radxa A7A docs
```

Inside the container, run the Allwinner-style export:

```bash
source ai-sdk/scripts/pegasus_setup.sh v3        # exports VSIMULATOR_*
ai-sdk/scripts/pegasus_import.sh   kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared
ai-sdk/scripts/pegasus_export_ovx_nbg.sh kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared
```

The `pegasus_export_ovx_nbg.sh` path produces the format the on-board
viplite v2.0.3 expects (we confirmed against `ai-sdk/examples/vpm_run/operator/v3/`,
which was produced by exactly this toolchain). The NBG will land at
`<...>_nbg/network_binary.nb`.

If this works, drop the resulting `.nb` into the existing
`models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs{1,4}.fp16/` dirs (replace `network_binary.nb`,
keep `nbg_meta.json`). The on-board C++ backend resolves the path
automatically — no code changes.

> **2026-04-29 amendment — what actually shipped.** Path A's image is
> not on Docker Hub at `radxa/ubuntu-npu:v2.0.10.1`. The image is on
> Allwinner's Synology netdisk; see `A733_CONVERSION.md §3.2` for the
> retrieval recipe. The `pegasus_setup.sh v3 / pegasus_export_ovx_nbg.sh`
> scripts in `ai-sdk/scripts/` expect a `_PLUS_` config name that
> doesn't exist in the v2.0.10.1 image; we drive `pegasus.py` directly
> with the no-`_PLUS_` name. See `tools/onnx_to_a733_docker.sh` for
> the actual working command sequence.

### Path B — bump `acuitylite` and re-export through `tools/onnx_to_a733.py`

If you'd rather stay on the pip wheel, the version in the alphazero env
that produced these NBGs emits NBG format `0x00020005` and writes only
the low byte of the chip ID. Try the latest published wheel:

```bash
pip install --upgrade acuitylite        # check version vs the one that emitted 0x20005
python tools/onnx_to_a733.py \
    --onnx models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared.onnx \
    --output-dir models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16 \
    --mode fp16
xxd models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb | head -1
```

After `xxd`, **check bytes 8..11 read `3b 00 00 10`** before shipping.
If they still read `15 00 00 00`, this version of acuitylite is also
ignoring `VSIMULATOR_CONFIG`; fall back to Path A.

`tools/onnx_to_a733.py` already sets the right env vars at the top of
the file (`VSIMULATOR_CONFIG=VIP9000NANODI_PLUS_PID0X1000003B`). Don't
remove that — newer acuitylite versions actually honor it.

> **2026-04-29 amendment — Path B is dead.** `acuitylite==6.51.0` (the
> latest on PyPI as of this writing) bundles a `libEmulator.so` whose
> chip table does not contain `0x1000003B`; setting `VSIMULATOR_CONFIG`
> has no effect, the codegen silently picks the generic VIP9000 default
> (low byte `0x15`). Verified across 6.42 / 6.45 / 6.48 / 6.51. There
> is no PyPI wheel that knows A733. Path A is the only way.

### Path C — diagnose acuitylite's chip-ID emission directly

If neither A nor B is convenient, check what acuitylite is doing. The
exporter is `acuitylib.interface.exporter.OvxlibExporter`; the chip ID
gets baked in by the OpenVX driver during the `pack_nbg_only=True` codegen
pass. With acuitylib loaded:

```python
import acuitylib, os
print(acuitylib.__file__)
print(acuitylib.__version__ if hasattr(acuitylib, "__version__") else "?")
# Look at the bundled prebuilt-sdk that does the codegen — the chip IDs
# are usually compiled into the simulator config tables there.
import os, pathlib
sdk = pathlib.Path(acuitylib.__file__).parent / "vsi_sdk"
print(list(sdk.rglob("*.so"))[:5])
```

The chip family table the codegen consults is generally inside
`vsi_sdk/prebuilt-sdk/x86_64_linux/lib/libArchModelSw.so`. If your
acuitylite predates A733 support, the table simply doesn't have
`0x1000003B` in it and the codegen silently picks a fallback (= the
`0x15` we're seeing).  This is the symptom — and the fix is "use a
toolchain that knows the chip", which loops back to Path A.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 6. Things that don't work (so you don't waste time on them)

### Patching the chip ID in the existing NBG

Tried it:

```bash
python3 -c "import struct; f=open('p.nb','r+b'); f.seek(8); f.write(struct.pack('<I',0x1000003B))"
xxd p.nb | head -1
# 56 50 4d 4e 05 00 02 00 3b 00 00 10 ...   ← header now matches
```

Then `vpm_run` against the patched file:

```
[0x88cfd750]vip_create_network[193], fail to create network status=-4
[0x88cfd750]viphal_os_call_drv[294], fail to ioctl vipcore, command[28]:VIPDRV_SET_TASK_PROPERTY, status=-3
```

Same `-4`. The chip ID is just one of several mismatches the validator
flags; the NBG also carries an older format-version preamble and the
underlying compiled command stream targets a different ISA variant. The
kernel ioctl rejects it independently of the header bytes.

### Running the conversion on the Cubie A7A board itself

`acuitylite` ships only x86_64 Linux binaries in its `prebuilt-sdk/`
(verified — the wheel layout is `acuitylib/vsi_sdk/prebuilt-sdk/x86_64_linux/`).
There is no aarch64 build. `qemu-user-static` x86_64 emulation can be
made to work for small NBGs but the Acuity simulator tends to hit
unsupported AVX/SSE codepaths under qemu and is ~10–30× slower; not
worth it when an x86 box is available.

### Switching to TIM-VX / TFLite as a workaround

TIM-VX is the open-source aarch64-native runtime, which would skip the
offline NBG step entirely — but the project's nn_server expects an
NBG-shaped backend (per-K precompiled, fixed batch, single
`predict_batch` call), and TIM-VX is a graph-JIT runtime. Adapting
would be a significant refactor and is out of scope. Better to fix
the NBG.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 7. Verification checklist before shipping new NBGs

After regenerating, run these on the Cubie A7A to confirm the NBG is
actually consumable by the on-board runtime. None of these need the
full minigo C++ build — they're all standalone:

1. **Header sanity** (offline, on the x86 host or the Cubie):

   ```bash
   python3 -c "
   import struct
   for p in ['kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb',
             'kata1-b10c128.a733.bs4.fp16/network_binary.nb']:
       with open(p,'rb') as f: h = f.read(12)
       magic, ver, target = struct.unpack('<III', h)
       print(f'{p}: magic={magic:#x} version={ver:#x} target={target:#x}')
   "
   ```

   Expected (with v6.30.22 in `ubuntu-npu:v2.0.10.1`):
   ```
   .../bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb: magic=0x4e4d5056 version=0x20000 target=0x1000003b
   .../bs4.fp16/network_binary.nb: magic=0x4e4d5056 version=0x20000 target=0x1000003b
   ```

2. **`vpm_run` smoke test on the Cubie** (no minigo dependency):

   ```bash
   ssh cubie
   cat > /tmp/smoke.txt <<EOF
   [network]
   /path/to/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.fp16/network_binary.nb
   EOF
   # 1782 fp16 spatial + 19 fp16 global = (22*9*9 + 19) * 2 = 3602 bytes
   head -c 3602 /dev/zero > /tmp/zeros.bin
   # vpm_run wants one input file per network input; bs=1 has two.
   # Easiest is to skip the input-comparison step:
   export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/root/proj/ai-sdk/viplite-tina/lib/aarch64-none-linux-gnu/v2.0
   /root/proj/ai-sdk/examples/vpm_run/install/etc/npu/vpm_run/vpm_run \
       -s /tmp/smoke.txt -l 5 -b 1 2>&1 | grep -E "create network|prepare network|profile inference|target=|status=|ret="
   ```

   Expected: lines matching `create network 0: NNN us`,
   `prepare network 0: NNN us`, several
   `profile inference time=NNNNus, cycle=NNNNNN`, and `vpm run ret=0`.
   Any line starting with `[0x...]nbglk_valid_nbg_check` is a fail.

3. **Full minigo benchmark** (after smoke passes):

   ```bash
   cd /root/proj/AlphaZero
   ./build/benchmark --model models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared.onnx \
       --max-batch 1 --nn-iters 100 --games 0 --threads 0
   ```

   Expected: prints `VIP9000 NBG header: target=0x1000003b version=0x20000`,
   followed by the standard "2. NN inference, single-thread (vip9000)..."
   timing block. ~3–5 ms per call would be in line with the ResNet-50
   measurements (~1 TOPS achieved on this NPU); kata1-b10c128 has fewer
   MACs than ResNet-50 so should be faster.

4. **Numerical parity** (existing tool, unchanged):

   ```bash
   python tools/a733_verify.py \
       --onnx models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared.onnx
   ```

   This is the host-side ORT-vs-simulator check — separate from the
   on-board run, but worth doing once after re-conversion.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 8. C++ runtime side — what's already wired up

For the converter side this is informational, but it's useful to know
exactly what the on-board side will do with your NBG so you don't have
to second-guess the integration:

* Build: `cmake .. -DMINIGO_BACKEND=vip9000 && make -j` on the Cubie.
  `VIPLITE_ROOT` defaults to `/root/proj/ai-sdk/viplite-tina`. Auto-detect
  picks `vip9000` if `/dev/vipcore` is present and `libNBGlinker.so` /
  `libVIPhal.so` are findable.

* NBG path resolution: `LoadedModel::model_path` is the source ONNX path
  (e.g. `models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs1.unshared.onnx`). The backend strips
  `.onnx`/`.unshared`/`.a733.bsN.fp16` suffixes, then tries
  `<base>.a733.bs<K>.fp16/network_binary.nb` where `K` is `1` if
  `--max-batch <= 1`, else `4`. So just dropping the regenerated NBGs
  back into the existing `models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs{1,4}.fp16/`
  directories is sufficient — no code changes needed.

* Threading: `--nn-server-threads N --nn-device-ids 0,0,...,0` (N copies
  of `0`) gives N concurrent server threads on the single A733 NPU. Each
  thread uses `vip_dup_network(VIP_DUP_FOR_CMD_BY_NETWORK)` so weight
  memory is shared (= one NBG worth of RAM for any thread count).

* Per-call: input fp32 from the encoder → host-side fp32→fp16 conversion
  into a mapped vip_buffer → `vip_flush_buffer(FLUSH)` →
  `vip_run_network` → `vip_flush_buffer(INVALIDATE)` → output fp16→fp32 →
  return. Padding for `N < model_batch` is in the input write loop;
  output slots `[N..K)` are dropped.

* I/O mapping: spatial vs global input is identified by name
  (`state_spatial` / `state_global`) with a 4-D-vs-2-D fallback. Outputs
  are mapped by name substring (`policy` / `value` / `score_mean` /
  `score_stdev` / `ownership`) with a position-based fallback (the
  ONNX-export order in `kata_export_for_rknn.py`). If the regenerated
  NBG keeps the same names it'll just work; if names change, the
  position fallback covers it as long as the order is preserved.

> **2026-04-29 amendment.** The C++ files this section references
> (`src/vip9000_compute.cpp`, the build wiring) are **not yet present**
> on `multi-gpu`. `git ls-tree -r multi-gpu | grep vip9000` returns
> nothing as of this date. The plan above is forward-looking — once
> the runtime patch lands, the NBGs in `models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs{1,4}.fp16/`
> are ready for it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 9. TL;DR

The converter is emitting an NBG with header `target=0x15,
version=0x20005`. The on-board viplite v2.0.3.2-AW-2024-08-30 wants
`target=0x1000003B, version=0x1001E`. Re-export with Allwinner's
official Acuity Toolkit (Path A above) or a `acuitylite` version that
honors `VSIMULATOR_CONFIG=VIP9000NANODI_PLUS_PID0X1000003B`. Verify
with `xxd <nbg> | head -1` (bytes 8..11 must be `3b 00 00 10`) and a
`vpm_run` smoke test on the Cubie before handing back. The C++ side is
done and waiting.

> **2026-04-29 amendment — current state.** Path A executed. NBGs at
> `models/kata1-b10c128.a733.bs{1,4}.fp16/network_binary.nb` now carry
> `target=0x1000003B, version=0x20000`. The version differs from the
> §9 claim above but matches Allwinner's own `yolact/v3` sample; the
> hard requirement is the target byte. `vpm_run` smoke test on the
> Cubie still pending — that's the next thing the converter side needs
> to hand back to the on-board side to confirm.
